          "derivation": "The Pahlavi word is genuine — MacKenzie p. 65: 'pās [pʾs | = M, N ~] guard, watch. ~ dāštan: keep watch, guard. pāsbān [pʾspʾn | M pʾsbʾn, N ~] guardian, watcher. ~īh: defence, protection.' It is pās 'watch' + the agent suffix -bān (< OIr. *-pāna- 'protecting'). What is disputed is the arrow: deriving pās/pāsbān from Avestan spas-. The standard Iranian handbooks derive pās from the root *peh₂- 'to protect' instead: Nyberg's glossary (II p. 159) states flatly 'pās [pʾs] watch, guard. — OP *pāça-, SW form of pāθra-, v. pahr. — MPrs pʾsbʾn \"watchman, guardian\"', i.e. MP pās goes back to an Old Persian *pāça- that is the south-western reflex of Avestan pāθra- 'protection'; Horn (no. 274, p. 62) has the same: 'pās \"Schutz\", pāsbān, pāsīden. aw. pāθra- \"Schutz\"; phlv. pās (Bund.), pāspān ... ai. pātra-.' Wiktionary follows them: 'From Middle Persian pʾs /pās/, from Old Persian *pāçah, from Proto-Iranian *páHθrah', ultimately PIE *peh₂- 'to protect'. On that view pās has nothing to do with spas- at all; the s of pās is from *-θr-, not the -s- of the spy-root, and the absence of initial s- is original, not a loss. Nourai is not alone, however: his source Tavoosi (SNS) does derive it from spas-, and Aryanpur's Persian IE dictionary (p. 151) files pās/pāsbān under Pokorny 984 while listing both PĀTHRA and SPASYEITI as Avestan comparanda — so a minority of Persian etymologists conflate the two roots. Modern scholarship prefers *pāθra-.",
